    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\nGM Prepares Hybrid Chevrolet Traverse, GMC Acadia, Buick Enclave\n6 photos\nPhoto: Chevrolet\nGeneral Motors spent one too many years telling American drivers that battery-electric vehicles are the future. As many of us knew from the very beginning, putting all eggs in one basket is a very poor tactic. Better late than never, Detroit's largest automaker has realized that giving its customer pool multiple powertrain choices is better than imposing electric nonsense on everyone.\nGM's mea culpa includes three hybrid sport utility vehicles, namely hybrid versions of the Chevrolet Traverse, GMC Acadia, and Buick Enclave. All three models would generate substantial business. According to Reuters, which cites people familiar with the automaker's domestic plans, all three will be produced at the Lansing Delta Township factory in Michigan.\nDesigned as the company's first green factory, LDT specializes in large crossovers. The complex operates as a complete production site, which means that LDT also handles metal stamping and body welding. The move to bring three hybrid crossovers to market will certainly make dealers happy, dealers who have been asking for hybrids for years now.\nGeneral Motors has confirmed an investment in the Lansing complex for hybrid production, but as opposed Reuters, the automaker hasn't yet identified the hybrid models planned for LDT. The launch schedule remains a mystery as well, and don't even dare ask about technical details.\nThere is, however, good reason to start with said three-row models. The Traverse, Acadia, and Enclave share much of their underpinnings. Chevrolet, as ever, handles the mainstream crowd, Buick is the premium alternative, and GMC mixes a premium character with rugged capability.\nPhoto: Chevrolet In combustion-only form, all three offer three-row seating. This makes fuel consumption a relevant concern. Families who spend plenty of time behind the wheel definitely prioritize efficiency, and hybridization is the right approach in this segment as of this writing.\nHybrid powertrains make large SUVs more appealing without asking customers to change how they use them. An internal combustion engine is still featured, there is no need to plug them in, and servicing costs aren't particularly different from purely internal combustion models.\nGM is understood to be developing both self-charging and plug-in hybrids. Chief executive officer Mary Barra has already underlined that GM needs a broader electrified offering, and the key word here is \"broader.\" The person in charge of General Motors also said that hybrids would cover multiple segments.\nMore curiously, Barra made it clear that hybrids wouldn't require additional engineering and capital expenditure beyond the company's existing plans. That seems a bit odd when you remember that Cadillac once intended to become fully and exclusively electric by 2030, but hey, who am I to question the CEO?\nTo support hybrid production at LDT, the automaker intends to redirect $500 million in federal funding originally awarded to support electric vehicle manufacturing in the state of Michigan. As if that wasn't enough, approximately $1.25 billion will be invested by General Motors in the Lansing site to accommodate hybrid vehicle production.\nVia // Reuters\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Chevrolet Traverse buick enclave Buick GMC Acadia GMC Hybrid US\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT First-Ever Supercharged Shelby Mustang GT350R Needs a New Owner, No Other Like It Exists",
